.TH VM 1
.SH NAME
vm \- run a command in another Dis virtual machine
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B vm
.br
.B vm
.I n command
[
.I arg ...
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
On the native kernel each processor core can run a Dis virtual machine of
its own (see
.IR vm (3)).
.I Vm
runs
.I command
in VM
.I n
and prints its pid; the command runs alongside whatever is running in the
other VMs, on another core, with
.IR vm 's
namespace, environment and file descriptors, so its output appears where
.IR vm 's
would.
.I Command
is a path, or the name of a module in
.BR /dis ,
as the shell finds commands.
.PP
With no arguments,
.I vm
prints how many VMs there are and, for each, its processes and heap.
.PP
Which VM a command goes to is the caller's choice.
VM 0 is the one everything runs in unless placed elsewhere, and the only
one with the window system.
A card can place programs at boot with a
.B vms
file; see
.IR osinit (8).
.SH EXAMPLES
Four compute-bound jobs, one a core:
.EX
	for i in 0 1 2 3 { vm $i myjob $i }
.EE
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/vm.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR vm (3),
.IR ps (1),
.IR kill (1)
.SH BUGS
A command run this way that loads
.B $Tk
or
.B $Draw
in any VM but 0 fails: those modules are VM 0's alone.
